welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Richard Ruby ascus professor of  anthropology and director of the Yale  reproductive ecology laboratory and the  why IBS program in reproductive ecology  professor Bree BS kisses most notable  research involves the evolutionary  biology and endocrinology of human and  comparative life histories reproduction  aging and metabolism he is the author of  men evolutionary and life history today  we talk with Professor bribie ascus  about the male species from a life  history and evolutionary perspective and  what it may hold for our future welcome  professor Velasquez Thank You Marilyn  thank you for having me you have spent a  good many years studying the human male  species and have arrived at a new way of  understanding them while we're pretty  much all familiar with evolutionary  theory it's the addition of the life  history theory that you bring that  provides a new approach tell us about it  well life history theory actually has a  pretty long history it's actually I  would say a part of evolutionary theory  and in basic terms it's sort of looking  at a species whether or not you're  looking you're thinking of humans or  fruit flies or whatever in terms of  specific events during the course of  their life such as when they're born how  long does it take for them to mature  when did it become reproductively mature  how long did they reproduce and  ultimately when do they die okay and we  know that a lot of the the timing of  those events are the results of  constraints constraints on your time  constraints on energy in terms of how  much food you can get from the  environment and what we're finding right  now is that life history theory can  inform us  in pretty powerful ways in terms of how  we we actually understand human biology  okay and what are some of the specific  things you look at to do that well one  of the ways we do this me and many of my  colleagues is to look at hormones and  what I tell my students is what what is  in anthropology is doing mucking around  with hormones but it has to do with the  fact that allah by allah g is the result  of the interaction between the  environment and your genes and hormones  are wonderful way of sort of looking to  that interaction they sort of there are  the liaison if you will of the  information that comes from the  environment your hormones sort of sense  is there a lot of food around is there  made around it's certainly your hormones  sort of adjust accordingly and then they  go in and sort of tinker with your genes  in hopefully an appropriate way for  whatever the environment is uh is  placing in front of you okay and after  all of this research that you've done  what can you tell us why are men the way  they are what are some of your  conclusions well men in contrast to  women have different constraints they  have different strains in their life and  those constraints are pretty common  across mammals they're not really  completely unique to humans there are  some unique things about humans but a  lot of the constraints are we see in  humans are common in other animals such  as the fact that metabolically one of  the biggest differences is that men or  male mammals period don't invest a lot  metabolically in reproduction men don't  gestate they don't lactate they don't  menstruate and so men don't have those  metabolic costs that women have to deal  with during the course of their life and  it really affects everything from their  behavior to their reproductive  strategies to their immune system so  those are some of some of the key  differences okay and what are some of  the things that may help us understand  how we will be in the future well what's  interesting is  if you understand the constraints that  women are under compared to men a lot of  it has to do with child rearing a lot of  it has to do with how much time you have  to spend raising your children and since  men can do a lot of things they can take  care of children I can do a lot of  things but they can't lactate at least  not under normal circumstances so what's  interesting is I think in the future  some of those constraints may be lifted  on women as they become more  economically and politically empowered  sometimes as we know having a male  partner is it even necessary to have a  child so I think what's interesting is  how the these these constraints that  were common throughout our evolution in  some instances aren't as relevant as  they were in the past I think they'll  still be things that will have to deal  with that it's interesting how I think  women in particular don't aren't under  the same constraints in many ways as  they were in the past do you think we'll  we'll find any new kinds of constraints  or difficulties moving forward with  these changes I think so I think  regardless of whether or not you have a  biological constraint there's always  economic constraints there's always  constraints on your time if you become  more economically empowered it comes at  a cost you have to spend more time at  work you invest more time with their  colleagues building relationships and  that's time spent away from your family  or your friends or whoever so there's  always going to be trade-offs which is  one of the to me one of the appeals of  life history theory because the core of  trade-offs applies even when you're  outside of the force if you're outside  of the which you think of natural  environment and in urban environment you  still have a lot of those constraints  and how does your theory apply to some  contemporary health concerns like  prostate cancer for instance or male  contraception hmm well in terms of we'll  start with with prostate cancer there  are costs and benefits to being in an  energy rich environment like we do so I  can leave the studio and walk down the  street and get a slice of pizza or have  it delivered and have lots of calories  with virtually no  effort and because I'm able to get those  calories I can afford to have higher  hormone levels like testosterone level  testosterone comes with a metabolic cost  and because it's higher I can build more  muscle if I wanted to if I want to  really hit the gym hard or something but  at the same time later on in life those  higher testosterone levels might  contribute to a higher risk of prostate  cancer since testosterone can increase  your risk of prostate cancer not at an  individual level but at a population  level in terms of things like a male  contraceptive I think it's interesting  to understand the motivation for men in  terms of why they would want to take a  male contraceptive women take  contraceptives because they want to  regulate the cost of benefits of having  children throughout their life it's a  good time at that time socially or  economically what have you mm-hmm for  men it's a little bit different since  they don't bear a lot of those costs  they don't again they don't gestate they  don't lactate so the motivation for  taking a male contraceptive maybe a  little bit different I think there may  be more of a market if you will if for  men who actually have a lot of research  wealthy men if you will and who may have  more of an incentive to I think to sort  of keep a closer eye if you will on  their reproduction okay and can you  speculate on how the human male species  will evolve down the road what do you  think we'll see Oh down the road if you  really consider some of my colleagues  some of the wonderful research you're  doing I think you're probably going to  see a lot more investment by males in  their offspring because I think part of  female empowerment is the fact that men  are going to have to negotiate with  women more about reproduction and a lot  of that negotiation is going to involve  investing more and children sticking  around help raising them investing in  them economically with their time their  resources so I think in the future if we  hopefully see more empowerment of women  I think you'll see men investing more in  childcare  more than you know even though humans  actually do invest a lot more in child  care than most other primates but I  think it's probably on the uptick okay  very good thank you so much for being  here today and sharing some of your  research thank you for more information  about professor Bree BS kiss and his  research please visit our website at  yale edu backslash Macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale
 

